# Rollback and Kill-Switch

## Enablement Precondition

No autonomous capability may be **enabled** until it satisfies both conditions:

(a) A **rollback path** to a known-good state is documented, written down before enablement, and tested.
(b) The capability **responds to the global kill-switch**, as defined in `core/governance/KILL-SWITCH.md`.

This is a structural precondition, not a post-build checklist item. A capability whose rollback path or kill-switch response is absent or untested is not "enabled with caveats" — it is not enabled.

**Grounding — the standing autonomy commitments:**

- **T1 surfaces never receive unattended autonomy.** The kill-switch is how that commitment is enforceable at runtime.
- **Parallel build and gated write-back gate on the precondition trio.** The parallel-build capability and gated write-back (the scheduled sentinel's phase-2) must not be enabled before all three enablement preconditions — credential-and-data isolation, spend governance, and this document — land. This document is the standard those capabilities must satisfy.
- **Parallel build carries "rollback-to-serial-by-default" as a mandatory property** — the first named instance of the rollback requirement defined below.

---

## Kill-Switch: Required Shape

The kill-switch for this project is defined in `core/governance/KILL-SWITCH.md`. Whatever its concrete form, the implementation must satisfy all of the following properties. The concrete halt action is the operator's to determine.

**Single action, total halt.**
One operator action halts all autonomous activity — scheduled runs, parallel builds, write-back — simultaneously. There is no per-capability whack-a-mole: a kill-switch that stops one automation while leaving another running is not a kill-switch.

**Fail-safe / fail-closed.**
When it is unclear whether automation is running, the kill-switch assumes it is and halts. The safe state is stopped, not running.

**Independent of the thing it kills.**
The kill path does not depend on the automation being responsive or healthy. A wedged or crashed autonomous process must not prevent the kill-switch from firing.

**Leaves the system in an inspectable state.**
After the kill-switch fires, the system is in a known, observable state — not an ambiguous mid-flight state requiring additional surgery to assess.

**Documented where the operator finds it under stress.**
The kill-switch is the operator's control, not a developer's tool. Its location, trigger, and confirmation are written in `core/governance/KILL-SWITCH.md`, where the operator reaches for it when something is wrong.

---

## Rollback: Required Shape

Every autonomous capability must define its rollback path before enablement. The path must satisfy all of the following:

**Revertible to a known-good state.**
Every change an autonomous capability can make is revertible to a state that was known-good before the capability ran. "Known-good" means a state the operator can describe and verify, not merely "before."

**Tested before enablement.**
The rollback path is exercised before the capability is enabled. Untested rollback is not rollback — it is a hypothesis. At minimum, the operator must demonstrate the revert works against a representative scenario before enabling the capability.

**Written before enablement.**
The rollback procedure is documented before the capability is turned on, not after the first incident. An undocumented rollback is unavailable under the stress conditions when it is needed.

**First named instance: parallel build's rollback-to-serial-by-default.**
The parallel-build capability's independence is never assumed: serial merge queue, auto-rebase/retest before merge, full post-merge regression gate, and rollback-to-serial-by-default are mandatory, not optional. When parallel build is built, its rollback path must meet the three requirements above, with the serial queue as the documented and tested known-good fallback. The concrete steps are authored when parallel build is built — they do not exist yet.

**Concrete steps are per-capability.**
The rollback procedures for the parallel-build capability and gated write-back (the scheduled sentinel's phase-2) are authored when those capabilities are built. This document is the standard they must meet; it does not substitute for the steps themselves.

---

## Forward Hooks

**Worktree-isolated parallel build:** must satisfy both the kill-switch shape and the rollback shape above before enablement. Rollback-to-serial-by-default is the required rollback instance; its tested form is authored at build time for that increment.

**Gated write-back (the scheduled sentinel's phase-2):** same requirement — kill-switch response and rollback path must both be satisfied before enablement.

**Read-only sentinel (the scheduled sentinel's phase-1):** the read-only sentinel may precede the full precondition regime. Even under that minimal gate, the kill-switch must be wired — a scheduled read-only process holds credentials and must be haltable. The minimal gate for phase-1 is defined in the operate-sentinel phase-1 recipe (`docs/recipes/operate-sentinel/sentinel-phase-1.RECIPE.md`, § The minimal gate).

**Sibling preconditions:** `core/methodology/least-privilege-isolation.md` defines what an enabled capability runs as and what it can reach, and `core/methodology/spend-governance.md` defines what it may spend; this document defines how it is stopped and reverted. A capability is safe to enable only when all three are satisfied — see `core/governance/KILL-SWITCH.md` and `core/governance/AUTOMATION-ISOLATION.md` together.
